# Response headers for Cloudflare Workers static assets.
#
# THIS FILE IS THE nginx SECURITY HEADERS, AGAIN, FOR A HOST THAT CANNOT READ
# THEM. web/nginx.conf and web/nginx-security-headers.conf were written for a
# container behind nginx. Cloudflare Pages does not run nginx and does not read
# either file, so on Pages every one of those headers is simply absent unless it
# is restated here. That is the whole failure mode this file prevents: the site
# keeps serving, looks identical, and ships with no CSP.
#
# The values below are byte-for-byte the ones in nginx-security-headers.conf, and
# TestThePagesHeadersRestateEverySecurityHeaderTheNginxConfigDeclares fails if
# the two ever disagree — in either direction, including a value edited on one
# side only. While both files exist they must say the same thing.
#
# Astro copies public/ verbatim into dist/, so this lands at dist/_headers, which
# is where Cloudflare looks. It is consumed rather than served as an asset.
#
# WORKERS APPENDS, PAGES OVERRODE, AND THAT DIFFERENCE WAS A LIVE BUG.
#
# This file used to set `Cache-Control: no-cache` on /* and rely on the later,
# more specific rules replacing it. That is Pages behaviour. On Workers Static
# Assets the values are CONCATENATED, so a hashed asset was served as
#
#   Cache-Control: no-cache, public, max-age=31536000, immutable
#
# and RFC 9111 gives `no-cache` the last word: revalidate before use. Every
# immutable asset asked the origin again on every navigation. Measured on the
# live site: 11 of 11 assets returning 304, and the render-blocking stylesheet
# costing ~588ms in front of first paint on each internal navigation.
#
# So no rule may set a header that a later rule also sets. The security headers
# stay on /* because they belong on everything; Cache-Control is now stated once
# per path class, and the HTML rule is scoped to the HTML rather than to /*.
# CLOUDFLARE WEB ANALYTICS IS NOT ALLOWED HERE, AND A TEST DECIDED THAT.
#
# Lighthouse (desktop, 2026-08-16) failed two audits on the live homepage --
# errors-in-console and inspector-issues -- for one reason:
#
#   Loading the script 'https://static.cloudflareinsights.com/beacon.min.js/...'
#   violates the following Content Security Policy directive:
#   "script-src 'self' 'unsafe-inline'"
#
# Cloudflare injects that beacon at the edge because Web Analytics is enabled on
# the Pages project. The first fix here added static.cloudflareinsights.com to
# script-src and cloudflareinsights.com to connect-src, and it was WRONG --
# TestThePagesHeadersRestateEverySecurityHeaderTheNginxConfigDeclares failed
# immediately, because this policy is required to match deploy/nginx.conf.example
# byte for byte. Its message is the whole argument:
#
#   "A header that is present and weakened is worse than one that is missing,
#    because it reads as covered."
#
# Matching the weakening into nginx would have been worse still: nginx never
# serves that beacon, so it would carry an exception for a script it cannot
# receive, purely to keep two files equal.
#
# THE FIX IS THEREFORE NOT HERE. Turn Web Analytics off in the Cloudflare
# dashboard. The beacon stops being injected, the console errors stop, and this
# policy stays as tight as the nginx one it mirrors. Nothing is lost: the
# analytics was collecting nothing anyway -- every request for it was blocked.
/*
  X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff
  Referrer-Policy: strict-origin-when-cross-origin
  Content-Security-Policy: default-src 'self'; img-src 'self' data:; style-src 'self' 'unsafe-inline'; script-src 'self' 'unsafe-inline'; font-src 'self'; base-uri 'self'; frame-ancestors 'none'

# HTML must revalidate, or a deploy is invisible until caches expire. Scoped to
# the built pages rather than /*, so it cannot leak onto a hashed asset.
#
# EVERY PAGE IS LISTED TWICE, AND BOTH SPELLINGS ARE LOAD-BEARING.
#
# astro.config.mjs sets `build.format: "file"` with `trailingSlash: "never"`, so
# a page is BUILT as features.html and SERVED as /features. `/*.html` matches the
# request path, not the file on disk -- so it caught none of the extensionless
# URLs anyone actually visits, and four of the six pages went out with no
# revalidation at all. The failure this block exists to prevent, "a deploy is
# invisible until caches expire", was happening on /features, /comparison, /docs
# and /download for as long as the rule has been here.
#
# The .html spellings stay because both resolve: a direct hit on /features.html
# is served rather than redirected, and a rule that covers only the pretty URL
# would leave the other door open.
#
# Adding a page means adding it here. That is deliberate -- broadening this to
# /* would reach /_astro/* below and turn immutable hashed assets into
# revalidate-every-time, which is the exact leak the original comment refused.
#
# /docs/* IS A PREFIX, AND IT IS THE ONE EXCEPTION TO "list every page".
#
# There are 23 rendered documentation pages under it and there will be more. A
# literal line each would be 23 lines nobody reads, that the next document
# silently does not get, and the failure mode is the one this block exists to
# prevent -- a deploy invisible until caches expire. A prefix cannot be
# forgotten. check-build.mjs matches built pages against these rules with prefix
# semantics for exactly this reason, so a page under /docs/ is covered and a new
# TOP-LEVEL page still has to be named here.
#
# It does not reach /_astro/*, which is the leak the paragraph above refuses --
# a Cloudflare `*` matches only within the segment prefix it follows.
#
# ON OVERLAPPING WITH /*.html, WHICH IT DOES. A request for /docs/obs.html
# matches both this rule and /*.html, and Workers CONCATENATES rather than
# replaces, so that response carries `Cache-Control: no-cache, no-cache`. That is
# fine and it is worth saying why, because the file's own rule is that no two
# rules may set the same header: the hazard being avoided is two rules with
# DIFFERENT values, where `no-cache, public, max-age=31536000, immutable` gave
# `no-cache` the last word and cost 11 of 11 assets a revalidation. Repeating one
# directive is idempotent under RFC 9111. The pretty URL -- the one anybody
# visits -- matches this rule alone.
/
  Cache-Control: no-cache
/features
  Cache-Control: no-cache
/comparison
  Cache-Control: no-cache
/docs
  Cache-Control: no-cache
/docs/*
  Cache-Control: no-cache
/download
  Cache-Control: no-cache
/free-restream-service
  Cache-Control: no-cache
/how-to-multistream-from-obs
  Cache-Control: no-cache
/multistream
  Cache-Control: no-cache
/twitch-vod-track
  Cache-Control: no-cache
/404
  Cache-Control: no-cache
# The comparison pages are nested, so they are SERVED at /vs/<name> and built as
# vs/<name>.html. Listed one per page for the same reason as the block above: a
# `/vs/*` wildcard would revalidate correctly today and silently stop being
# checkable, because the build guard matches a rule against each built page's
# exact served path.
/vs/aitum-multistream
  Cache-Control: no-cache
/vs/obs-multi-rtmp
  Cache-Control: no-cache
/vs/restreamer
  Cache-Control: no-cache
/vs/streamelements
  Cache-Control: no-cache
/vs/streamlabs
  Cache-Control: no-cache
/*.html
  Cache-Control: no-cache

# Content-hashed by the build, so it can be cached hard and forever. Same
# reasoning as the /_astro/ location in nginx.conf.
/_astro/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=31536000, immutable

# Stable but NOT content-hashed: a week, so replacing one reaches people.
# Mirrors nginx.conf's 7d on woff2/png/jpg/svg/webm/mp4.
#
# /shots/* USED TO BE HERE and no longer needs to be. The screenshots moved into
# src/assets so the build can emit responsive variants of them, which means they
# come out content-hashed under /_astro/ and are covered by the immutable rule
# above -- a strictly better answer than a week, because a replaced screenshot
# gets a new filename and reaches people immediately rather than after the TTL.
/fonts/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=604800
